WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.630 --> 00:00:04.293
- [Raimundo] Puerto Rico's my home.

00:00:05.410 --> 00:00:08.960
I've really devoted a
lot of my life and career

00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:11.260
to protecting Puerto
Rico's natural resources.

00:00:12.650 --> 00:00:16.440
So when Hurricane Irma
and Maria hit Puerto Rico,

00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:19.550
there was devastation
throughout the entire island.

00:00:19.550 --> 00:00:21.160
And so this is something that I felt that

00:00:21.160 --> 00:00:24.223
we really had to address and take action.

00:00:31.960 --> 00:00:33.780
Commercial fishermen in Puerto Rico

00:00:33.780 --> 00:00:36.543
are providing the base to
a lot of economic growth.

00:00:38.090 --> 00:00:40.660
While every industry was really affected,

00:00:40.660 --> 00:00:42.810
fishing communities
were severely impacted.

00:00:45.210 --> 00:00:48.910
A lot of fishing gear that was near shore

00:00:48.910 --> 00:00:51.523
was pulled back into the
ocean by the hurricane.

00:00:52.750 --> 00:00:55.470
This is something that was
of great personal concern

00:00:55.470 --> 00:00:57.893
just because this causes
them to be ghost traps.

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:02.210
Ghost traps are a real problem
for the marine environment

00:01:02.210 --> 00:01:04.220
because they continued fishing.

00:01:04.220 --> 00:01:08.410
So they're catching fish and
other species that die off,

00:01:08.410 --> 00:01:10.240
serve no purpose for
the marine environment,

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:12.440
and have no benefit to
commercial fishermen.

00:01:13.410 --> 00:01:15.360
We really wanted to see
how we could address

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:18.480
helping out fishing communities
while at the same time

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:20.430
cleaning up this derelict fishing gear.

00:01:26.460 --> 00:01:27.700
We really wanted to collaborate

00:01:27.700 --> 00:01:29.840
with dive fishermen in Puerto Rico.

00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:32.010
Because of this incredible knowledge

00:01:32.010 --> 00:01:35.550
that they have that nobody
else has of the sea floor.

00:01:35.550 --> 00:01:39.320
And because we wanted to
provide an alternative income

00:01:39.320 --> 00:01:41.280
during the aftermath of Hurricane Maria

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:42.993
to really help them move forward.

00:01:45.270 --> 00:01:47.200
When the fishermen go out fishing,

00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:49.600
they're underwater collecting fish.

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:50.750
And while they're down there,

00:01:50.750 --> 00:01:53.670
they identify where
ghost traps are located,

00:01:53.670 --> 00:01:56.069
and then they'll give me a call.

00:01:56.069 --> 00:01:56.902
(splashing)

00:01:56.902 --> 00:01:58.420
So I'll come out the next day or two

00:01:58.420 --> 00:01:59.970
and start pulling up this gear.

00:02:01.600 --> 00:02:03.520
The fishermen are
compensated for their time,

00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:06.470
and they're really brought as
collaborators on the project.

00:02:07.370 --> 00:02:09.360
We covered all of Puerto Rico.

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:11.520
It's just over 15 communities

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:13.613
that have participated in this effort.

00:02:20.410 --> 00:02:24.400
We've been able to remove
just under 400 ghost traps,

00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:27.770
and it totals up to 7 tons
of derelict fishing gear

00:02:27.770 --> 00:02:30.143
that we've removed from
Puerto Rico's ocean.

00:02:33.090 --> 00:02:34.890
After the impact of Hurricane Maria,

00:02:34.890 --> 00:02:37.310
we really felt alone.

00:02:37.310 --> 00:02:40.743
But it really brought a lot
of Puerto Rico together.

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:44.360
Without the local knowledge
of these fishermen,

00:02:44.360 --> 00:02:46.100
we would not have been
able to come together

00:02:46.100 --> 00:02:49.680
as a people to really
survive and move forward

00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:51.610
to see how things can improve here,

00:02:51.610 --> 00:02:54.600
and it's something that we
need to continue recognizing.

00:02:54.600 --> 00:02:56.950
Not just for fishing communities,

00:02:56.950 --> 00:02:59.973
but for all response to emergencies.

00:03:05.306 --> 00:03:07.473
(chiming)

